(a) It shall be unlawful for any person to create or maintain a nuisance on property owned or occupied by such person. For purposes of this division, the term "nuisance" is defined to mean an act or condition that causes hurt, inconvenience, or damage to another, and the fact that the act done may otherwise be lawful shall not keep it from being a nuisance. A nuisance includes the following conditions when they pose a detriment or danger to the public health, safety, or general welfare as determined by the county administrator or his designee:
(1) Any dead or decaying matter, including but not limited to: plants, fruits, vegetables, and animals;
(2) Pollution of public water or the injection of harmful or dangerous substances into the sewage system;
(3) Obstruction of a public street, highway or sidewalk without lawful authority;
(4) Any building or structure which, due to deteriorating or damaged condition endangers persons or property, including but not limited to those conditions set forth in O.C.G.A. § 41-2-7, as it may be amended;
(5) Any tree or other vegetation that due to its condition or location endangers persons or property, including but not limited to when such vegetation obstructs the clear and safe view of traffic, obstructs or damages public sidewalks or rights-of-way, endangers a public drainage system, or is in danger of falling;
(6) Machinery, equipment, or other tangible item that is in a dilapidated, wrecked, junked, dismantled, or inoperative condition and is not completely enclosed within a building or a dwelling;
(7) Pollution of the air by gases, vapors, fumes, mists, dusts, or smoke in quantities sufficient to be disagreeable, discomforting, or detrimental in relation to the conditions of the surrounding area;
(8) Filthy water or excrement, broken or ruptured sewer lines, raw sewage, polluted water from kitchens, bathrooms, laundries, privies, stables, or other such places, and any combination thereof;
(9) Trash, garbage, litter, or miscellaneous refuse permitted or caused to accumulate in any unauthorized manner;
(10) Growth of grass or weeds on any property to a height of 24 inches or more, including but not limited to when such condition creates a breeding place for noxious insects, rodents, or reptiles, or constitutes a fire hazard; and
(11) Any other condition constituting a nuisance under state law.
(b) The provisions of subsection (a) of this section shall not be applicable to agricultural facilities, agricultural operations, and agricultural support facilities, as those terms are defined by O.C.G.A. § 41-1-7, operated in accordance with applicable industry standards and practices and otherwise in compliance with the provisions of this Code and state law.
(Ord. No. 2017-005, § 30-71, 5-9-2017)

